        'id': 814,
        'slug': 'Microwave_Transceiver',
        'title': 'Microwave Transceiver',
        'author': 'Myiven',
        'lastUpdated': '2007-08-06 14:05:01',
        'categories': [
            'Communications',
            'Engineering Systems'
        ],
        'rawContent': "{{Weapon Information|\n|name = Microwave Transceiver\n|type = Tactical Communications System\n}}\n\nFor orbit/surface communications, many designs use tight-beam microwaves to carry information.\n\nMicrowaves can be intercepted, so commanders use two methods of encryption for their information. First, for the most important and sensitive orders, they use One Time Pad (OTP) encryption. OTP is theoretically unbreakable, but no Pad (base for encryption/decryption) can be used more than once. Surface commanders and orbiting commanders must periodically ensure that they have identical Pads, and agree on an order for use. These Pads are carried by armed courier from surface to orbit. If there's any possibility that Pad security has been compromised, the entire batch is destroyed and replaced.\n\nSecond, for less vital orders, they use Vernam ciphers. Vernam ciphers use a random encryption method that requires a key. Like the OTP system, keys are sent to battlefield commanders beforehand, but new ones are transmitted via OTP encryption rather than armed courier.\n\nThe Microwave Transceiver is the most common use of orbit/surface and surface/surface communications. The range is determined by the wattage of the transmitter, and the clarity of the signal (and resistance to jamming) is determined by the frequency.\n\nTypically, military communications ranges from 25-115GHz (though dedicated stations can make it to 500GHz), and their power output depends on the size of the unit, from small 1mW infantry transmitters to 600gW planetary broadcast stations.\n\nBelow is a listing of all models in use by humans throughout the galaxy:\n*Achernar Air Whistler\n*Achernar Electronics HICK-4\n*Achernar Electronics HICS-11\n*Achernar Electronics HID-21\n*Angst Clear Channel 3\n*Beacon Barret Party Line-200\n*Basix 200\n*CeresCom 8\n*CeresCom 21-Rs\n*CeresCom 37-P\n*CeresCom Recon 12K\n*Ceres Metals 666\n*Colmax 90\n*Colmax OmniComm 5C\n*Colmax OmniComm 8D\n*CommuTech Multi-Channel 10\n*CommuTech XL\n*ComStar Rover\n*COMSTAT 400-D\n*COMSTAT 500 ATM\n*Corean TransBand-J9\n*CurtissComm Mark I\n*Cyclops 1\n*Cyclops 12\n*Cyclops 14\n*Dalban Micronics\n*Dalban StarLink Classic\n*Dalban StarLink II\n*Datacom 26\n*Datacom 50\n*Datacom 60\n*Dec-10 Whisperer\n*Duoteck 20\n*Duoteck 65\n*Duoteck 95\n*Duoteck 100\n*EasyTalk-4\n*Endicott Type 22 Maser\n*Exeter LongScan 200 ReconLook\n*Faulk 203 Comset\n*Garret Supremesound\n*Garret T10B\n*Garret T11-A\n*Garret T11-b\n*Garret T12-A\n*Garret T12E\n*Garret T19-G\n*Garret Type 4\n*Hartford JEA-1\n*HartfordCo COM 4000\n*Irian E.A.R.\n*Irian Orator-5K\n*Irian Technologies HMR-35s\n*Irian TelStar\n*Irian UHF\n*Johnston Q-Band\n*Johnston Q-Rotor 2\n*Johnston Wide Band\n*JoLex Systems\n*Kallon Secure Net\n*Lassitor-3QS\n*Lassitor-3QZ\n*Lassitor FibroLink\n*Lockheed-CBM COMSET 100\n*Lockheed Matrix-V\n*LongCom Lynx-shur\n*Magestrix Alpha\n*Magestrix Beta\n*Marshall 333\n*Marshall Long-Talk\n*Maxell 500\n*Maxell 550\n*Maxim New Standard I-O\n*Nashan Quartet ComSy\n*Neil 2000\n*Neil 4000\n*Neil 5000\n*Neil 6000\n*Neil 6000-x\n*Neil 8000\n*Neil 9000\n*Neil 9000-A\n*Olmstead 30\n*Olmstead 37\n*Olmstead 3000\n*Omicron 1500 Channel\n*O-P 300 COMSET\n*O-P 3000 COMSET\n*O-P 3000 OSJ\n*O-P 9000AT\n*O-P AIR500\n*O-P AIR900\n*O-P COM-22-H47\n*O-P COM-211\n*O-P COM-500\n*O-P COMNET 201A\n*O-P COMSET\n*OP-R Janxiir\n*Orbitcom 11\n*Pauley-Bronson Z\n*Rander 100\n*Rander 100B\n*Rander 200\n*Rander 300\n*Scarborough Talky-1\n*Scarborough Talky-2\n*Scarborough Talky-3\n*Scuti Dualcom\n*Sipher CommCon SCU-4\n*Sipher CommSys 1\n*Sipher CommSys 3\n*Sipher CommSys 4\n*Sipher Security Plus\n*Tek BattleCom\n*Telestar F-67\n*Telestar XTD-67A1\n*TharHes Calliope Hm-10\n*TharHes Calliope ZE-2\n*TharHes Crystal Flower RG-2\n*TharHes Euterpe HM-14\n*TharHes HQ CommSet\n*TharHes Kr-A P-comm\n*TharHes Mini-Talk\n*TharHes Muse 54-58K\n*TharHes Thalia HM-22\n*Tri-Word Duplex 4880\n*Winston MegaBoozie Mark IX\n*Winston Silicon Siren Wonder-Talk VI\n*Wunderland XXV-2 Series\n*Wunderland XXXV-1 Series\n*Xilex-2000\n*Xilex-2010\n\n[[Category:Communications]]\n[[Category:Engineering Systems]]"
